hi I'm Lisa Manning I'm an assistant  professor at Syracuse University  so we've been interested in trying to  understand how groups of cells interact  with one another mechanically to  generate properties of biological  tissues and processes like development  this is really important because it  turns out that lots of cells have  signaling molecules that can measure  things about their mechanical  environment and respond to it and that's  one way of programming interesting  patterns during development and in  particular we've been interested in  understanding whether groups of cells  behave more like a fluid or more like a  solid and it turns out that the tools of  Glassie physics of glassy material  science help us to understand how groups  of disordered things whether they be  atoms or they B cells can transition  from a fluid like behavior to a solid  like behavior and so we've been thinking  about this problem and we've learned  that when groups of cells change single  cell properties things like the number  of cat hearings which is the adhesive  molecules that help cells stick together  or the expression levels of actin or  myosin which help give the cells  mechanical integrity they can actually  drive the entire tissue from a fluid to  a solid and those transitions can help  shape organs into functional structures  and they can help change the way the  tissue responds to mechanical forces and  so we think that this shows a lot of  promise for understanding how organs  change their shape during development  with implications for congenital disease
 

